1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Least privilege: roles and grants. Give the role its job, then prove it can't do more

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:11,500
A NOLOGIN role has no password. It only collects privileges.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,300
Membership lets your login act as the role for a test: SET LOCAL ROLE.

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:20,300
Then session_user is still your login; current_user is metro_analyst.

5
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:25,000
The analyst needs ticket summaries. Not emails, not notes, no edits.

6
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,700
USAGE on the schema only lets the role look up objects inside it.

7
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:36,700
SELECT on the view, and nothing on the base tables.

8
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:40,200
Allowed. The view reads tickets using its OWNER's privileges, not the analyst's.

9
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:47,700
That is what lets a view expose chosen columns without a grant on the table.

10
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:53,500
Denied: permission denied for table users, SQLSTATE 42501.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,500
Denied again. Inside BEGIN ... ROLLBACK, even a wrong grant changes nothing.

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,600
42501 is the passing result. A typo or a network error is not.

13
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:09,200
Suppose SELECT on tickets had also been granted to PUBLIC.

14
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:12,900
Revoking the direct grant leaves the PUBLIC path. Trust the test result, not the REVOKE.

15
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:24,500
Remember: A NOLOGIN group role holds privileges; logins become members. Reaching a table needs schema USAGE plus a privilege on the object. A normal view checks base tables with its OWNER's privileges. Test one allowed AND one denied action. Denied = SQLSTATE 42501. Privileges can also arrive through memberships and PUBLIC.

